@wilkinsona Thanks, fixed.

@vpavic gRPC might be the default for the OTel agent but I think there are a few things here we need to consider (apart the fact that the agent is not used here):
- As far as I remember for the non-agent use-case HTTP/protobuf is the default: https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-java/pull/3786.
- The OTel Java SDK used to use gRPC by default but there were lots of issues: users running into dependency conflicts and they needed to bring and configure their own gRPC lib eventually. See https://github.com/spring-projects-experimental/spring-cloud-sleuth-otel/issues/39 and https://github.com/open-telemetry/opentelemetry-java/issues/3123#issuecomment-814577392.
- Since OTel SDKs do not need to support all three transports, the OTel Collectors must (to support all SDKs) which means no-one is forced (by the collectors) to use a certain transport. In fact, the Java SDK does not support HTTP/JSON and it is not planned that it will be ever supported (See [CNCF Slack](https://cloud-native.slack.com/archives/C014L2KCTE3/p1677780415787389)).

Because of these I'm tending towards not adding support for gRPC and let the users configure it if they want to. We can always revisit this if there will be user need for gRPC (FWIW, we did not see this ask with Micrometer's OTLP registry, neither with Sleuth OTel after OTel SDK moved to HTTP/protobuf).

